%N A402288 Triangle read by rows: among the lists of n positive integers that contain no earlier row as a subsequence, row n has the smallest possible largest term and, among those, is the lexicographically smallest.
%C A402288 A list u is a subsequence of a list w if u can be obtained from w by deleting terms, adjacent or not, without changing the order of the remaining ones: 2, 3 is a subsequence of 2, 4, 3 but not of 3, 2. The largest term is minimized first; then, of two lists, the lexicographically smaller is the one with the smaller term at the first position where they differ. Lists of n terms that contain no earlier row always exist: n copies of a number larger than all earlier terms form one.
%C A402288 The largest term of a row is never smaller than that of the row before and exceeds it by at most 1 (proved in the notes file). So the largest term of row n equals that of row n-1 if some list of n numbers, each at most that largest term, contains no earlier row; otherwise it is one more. By Higman's lemma (1952), every infinite sequence of lists over a finite set of numbers has a list that is a subsequence of a later one, so the rows cannot use a finite set of numbers forever; since the largest term grows by at most 1 at a time, every positive integer appears.
%C A402288 The numbers 1, 2, 3, 4 first appear in rows 1, 2, 3, 7. The number 5 first appears in row 2847103814622277, which is 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4 followed by 5's, and the number 6 first appears after row 2^(2^2847103814622274) (all proved in the notes file); the exact row of the first 6 is not known. A402289(k) is the row in which k first appears.
%C A402288 The notes file proves that from row 15 through row 2847103814622276, each row consists of j 3's, then one or more 4's, then possibly an ending, which is a list of 2's, 3's and 4's that begins with 2 or 3. These rows form six consecutive blocks: row 15 alone, with j = 5 and no ending, and then the rows with j = 4, 3, 2, 1, 0, which are rows 16 to 26, 27 to 170, 171 to 12350, 12351 to 75464133 and 75464134 to 2847103814622276, respectively. Each of the last five blocks uses up a finite list of 10, 143, 12179, 75451782 and 2847103739158142 endings, respectively: one ending in each row, longer endings first, and then one last row without an ending. For example, the last block consists of rows 75464134 to 75464134 + 2847103739158142 = 2847103814622276. Rows 15 to 26 are worked out in the Example section.
%C A402288 Both parts of the rule matter. Without minimizing the largest term first, the rows would be 1; 2, 2; 2, 3, 3; 2, 3, 4, 4; 2, 3, 4, 5, 5; and so on (proved in the notes file). If, still with the smallest possible largest term, the lexicographically largest list is taken instead of the smallest, then the numbers 1, 2, 3 still first appear in rows 1, 2, 3, and for 4 <= k <= 14 the number k first appears in row 3*2^(k-4) + 1 = A004119(k-3) (proved in the notes file for k <= 8, computed for 9 <= k <= 14).
%C A402288 The rows form a sequence of lists in which no list is a subsequence of a later one. Friedman (2001) studied how long such a sequence can be when only k different numbers occur and the i-th list has at most i+1 terms; here, instead, the i-th list has i terms and is the greedy choice of the definition. A094091 and A093382 concern a related problem of his, in which the lists are the blocks x(i), ..., x(2i) of one sequence x of 0's and 1's. In A402229 the n-th term is the largest n-digit number from which no earlier term can be obtained by deleting digits; A402230 restricts which digits may be deleted. See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
